\f0\fs22 \cf0 1729 is known as the Hardy-Ramanujan number after a famous anecdote of the British mathematician G. H. Hardy regarding a hospital visit to the Indian mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan. In Hardy's words:\
\
It is the smallest number expressed as the sum of 2 cubes in 2 different ways: \
	1729 = 1\super 3\nosupersub  + 12\super 3\nosupersub  = 9\super 3\nosupersub  + 10\super 3\nosupersub \
\
it is the third Carmichael number & the first absolute Euler pseudoprime.\
\
It is divisible by the sum of its digits & thus a Harshad number; it also works in octal & hexadecimal\
\
The 1729th place of the trancendental number e is the first occurrence of all 10 digits consecutively: 0719425863\
\
It is one of 4 natural numbers (1, 81, 1458, 1729) which, when digits are added together, produces a sum, which when multiplied by its reversal, yields the original number: 	\
	1+7+2+9 = 19; 19*91 = 1729\
\
